in july and august temperatures dipped  below freezing in parts of brazil  causing the worst frost in decades  fields that used to look like this  quickly turned to this  now months later you may notice the  price of your coffee going up so how did  one bad weather event in one region  rattle the entire global coffee market  let's find out  brazil grows more coffee than any other  country and it produces about a third of  what we drink here in the u.s  and that area that was hit by the frost  it's considered the most important  coffee growing region in the country  gustavo guimaras is a brazilian  agronomist who's worked in the coffee  industry for more than two decades  this was the worst  frost in my  professional life i saw so far over  whatsapp he showed us these fields full  of dead and browned trees he says the  younger trees were completely destroyed  and will have to be taken out of the  ground and some of the older trees had  to be cut all the way down to the stumps  yeah so we had to come with them like a  chainsaw and cut it out  so a new tree will come out  and this would take like  two more years to start producing  and then he showed us the many other  trees that were damaged but not  destroyed they'll produce coffee next  year but not nearly as much as they were  expected to  these were the branches that we were  expecting to have coffee  in 2022 to make matters worse the frost  followed a bad drought in brazil so the  trees were already suffering  gustavo says it's possible that brazil  will lose 10 percent of its harvest next  year  to put that in perspective that  basically equals 20  of what americans drink in a year  how many years do you think it will take  brazil to bounce back from this  tough question  i would say at least at least three  years  three years if the weather is good  exactly  so clearly there's a coffee crisis in  brazil but why is it a big deal for the  global coffee industry and all of us  coffee drinkers for that i called carlos  mara he's been researching the coffee  industry for 15 years and his name  frequently pops up in journal articles  he says the bean that's been affected in  brazil is the same kind of bean that's  most commonly consumed here in america  brazil  produces mainly arabica which is the  type of coffee that you're likely to  find in a sort of mid-range to upper  range  plan  in supermarkets but also in coffee shops  adding to the problem is the timing of  the frost arabica beans naturally cycle  between weak and strong years and this  was already supposed to be a weak year  so farmers were banking on next year's  harvest to boost their stockpiles  that's a big reason why the market  reacted sharply when the frost hit the  price of arabica beans soared to six  year highs as traders worried about a  lack of supply  those prices have remained high and now  you may start to notice them being  passed along to you depending on where  and how you drink your coffee giant  coffee companies like starbucks buy  their coffee more than a year in advance  and so far starbucks has said it's not  worried about its supply but small and  mid-sized coffee companies can't afford  to buy all their coffee that far in  advance and they're definitely feeling  the pinch  owners of coffee companies that i talked  to said brazilian beans are 75 percent  more expensive than they were just a few  months ago and they say that what  happens in brazil doesn't stay in brazil  if there's a shortage in brazil then  demand for coffee from other countries  rises and so do the prices  on top of rising costs coffee importers  and roasters are dealing with the same  supply chain headaches and rising  shipping costs that are affecting other  industries and those factors are pushing  prices higher too  so what does this mean for you and me  and the millions of people who became  work from home baristas over the last  year and a half  well price hikes will be most noticeable  for those of us who brew our own 